\section{Model}\label{sec:model}

\paragraph{Network.} A finite directed acyclic graph $G=(V,E)$, where
$u\to v$ means $u$ supplies $v$. A focal node $0$ consumes $K_0$ input
categories. Every node $v$ consumes categories $k\in K_v$, each served by a
set of suppliers. Service is conjunctive across categories and substitutable
within a category, in the sense that any one working supplier suffices. That
clause fixes the semantics of the cascade rather than the sourcing structure.
The candidate set is epistemic rather than a redundancy: exactly one candidate
actually supplies the category, and the set records who it might be. The
model therefore contains no concurrently multi-sourced category, and dual
sourcing as a mitigation lies outside it. Each node has a publicly known securing cost $c_v$ and
belongs to a publicly known correlation cluster. A node's \emph{tier} is its
distance from the focal node along the supply direction: the focal firm's
direct suppliers are tier one, their suppliers tier two, and so on.

\paragraph{Uncertainty.} The structure parameter $\theta$ specifies, for
each latent category $(v,k)$, which candidate in a finite set $C_{v,k}$ is
the true supplier, exactly one candidate serving the category, an assumption
Corollary~\ref{cor:dilution} and Proposition~\ref{prop:disp} use and, in the dispersed regime of
Section~\ref{sec:measure}, which candidate carries which risk attribute. A
prior $\pi$ over $\theta$ is given. Probing a node reveals its candidate
in-edges through a noisy asymmetric channel. That channel belongs to the
wider model, of which this paper studies only the value of the information a
probe could deliver; it enters neither $\evpi$ nor the quantity measured in
Section~\ref{sec:measure}, since the uninformed planner sees only the prior
and the informed planner sees $\theta$ outright.

\paragraph{Disruption and loss.} A scenario $\omega$ draws node-level
failures, correlated within clusters by a one-factor Gaussian copula.
Operability propagates through the conjunctive cascade. A plan
$S\subseteq V\setminus\{0\}$ with $c(S)\le B$ qualifies backup sources:
$u\in S$ guarantees $u$'s output to its consumers. The focal loss is the
fraction of focal categories starved,
\begin{equation}
L(S;\theta,\omega)=\frac{1}{K_0}\sum_{k=1}^{K_0} Z_k,
\qquad Z_k=\mathbf{1}\{\text{focal category }k\text{ starved}\}.
\label{eq:loss}
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Objectives and the exact target.} Write
$j(S;\theta)=\mathbb{E}_\omega L(S;\theta,\omega)$ and
$J(S;\theta)=\cvar_{\alpha,\omega}(L(S;\theta,\omega))$. Let $S_\pi$
minimise $\mathbb{E}_{\theta\sim\pi}j(S;\theta)$ and $S_\theta$ minimise
$j(S;\theta)$. Then
\begin{equation}
\evpi(\pi)=\mathbb{E}_\pi\!\left[j(S_\pi;\theta)\right]
-\mathbb{E}_\pi\!\left[j(S_\theta;\theta)\right]\;\ge\;0 .
\label{eq:evpi}
\end{equation}

\paragraph{Units.} $L$ is a fraction of focal categories, so every quantity
here is dimensionless. Because $L$ is normalised by $K_0$, and $K_0$ runs from $2$ to $672$ across
the retained chains, the same absolute value means very different things on
different chains: rescuing one focal category moves the objective by
$1/K_0$, which is $0.019$ at the median $K_0=52$ and $0.0015$ at $K_0=672$.
An advantage of $0.0011$ on a chain at the median $K_0$ would thus be about a
twentieth of one focal category. That is an illustration at one value of
$K_0$, not the measurement. The measurement is the scaled quantity
$H\cdot K_0$, which says directly how many focal categories
perfect structural knowledge rescues: across the benchmark instances it
averages $0.020$ at $\lambda=0$, with a median of exactly zero and a maximum
of $1.012$, and $0.146$ at $\lambda=0.8$, with a median of $0.018$ and a
maximum of $2.379$. Perfect knowledge therefore rescues on average a fiftieth
of one focal category in the public-attribute regime and about a seventh of
one in the latent-attribute regime, and at most $1.01$ and $2.38$
respectively. The illustration and the measurement differ by about a factor of
three at $\lambda=0$ because $H$ is smaller on the chains with large $K_0$;
Section~\ref{sec:open} reports how the scaled quantity varies with $K_0$. A value of
$0.005$ means that perfect structural knowledge would reduce the objective by
half a percentage point of the focal basket: expected category starvation where the objective is $j$, and the
conditional tail average of the starved fraction where it is $J$, as it is in
the measurements of Section~\ref{sec:measure}. Whether that is a large amount of money depends
on what one starved category costs the firm, which this model does not
represent; Section~\ref{sec:money} gives the conversion a reader needs to
price it for a specific firm.

\subsection{Three quantities that must not be conflated}
\label{sec:threequantities}

The empirical sections do not measure \eqref{eq:evpi}. A firm operates an
implemented planner rather than an exact optimiser, and so do we. Let
$\hat S_\pi$ and $\hat S_\theta$ be the plans returned by the implemented
planner given the prior and given $\theta$. Define the \emph{heuristic
oracle advantage}
\begin{equation}
H \;=\; \mathbb{E}_\pi\!\left[j(\hat S_\pi;\theta)\right]
      -\mathbb{E}_\pi\!\left[j(\hat S_\theta;\theta)\right].
\label{eq:H}
\end{equation}

\begin{proposition}[Exact relation between $H$ and EVPI]\label{prop:H}
Let $\varepsilon_\pi=\mathbb{E}_\pi[j(\hat S_\pi;\theta)]
-\mathbb{E}_\pi[j(S_\pi;\theta)]\ge0$ and
$\varepsilon_\theta=\mathbb{E}_\pi[j(\hat S_\theta;\theta)
-j(S_\theta;\theta)]\ge0$ be the planner's optimality gaps in the two
regimes. Then
\begin{equation}
H=\evpi(\pi)+\varepsilon_\pi-\varepsilon_\theta .
\label{eq:Hrelation}
\end{equation}
Hence $H\le\evpi$ if and only if $\varepsilon_\pi\le\varepsilon_\theta$. In
general the sign of $\varepsilon_\pi-\varepsilon_\theta$ is not determined,
so $H$ is neither an upper nor a lower bound on EVPI; if the planner is
$\theta$-optimal when given $\theta$, so that $\varepsilon_\theta=0$, then
$H\ge\evpi$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
(E1) By definition of $\varepsilon_\pi$,
$\mathbb{E}[j(\hat S_\pi;\theta)]=\mathbb{E}[j(S_\pi;\theta)]
+\varepsilon_\pi$.

\noindent(E2) By definition of $\varepsilon_\theta$,
$\mathbb{E}[j(\hat S_\theta;\theta)]=\mathbb{E}[j(S_\theta;\theta)]
+\varepsilon_\theta$.

\noindent(E3) Subtracting (E2) from (E1) and using \eqref{eq:evpi} gives
\eqref{eq:Hrelation}. Both gaps are nonnegative because $S_\pi$ and
$S_\theta$ minimise over the same feasible sets that the planner searches
heuristically, and neither gap dominates the other in general, which gives
the remaining statements.
\end{proof}

Proposition~\ref{prop:H} holds for any fixed objective, provided the same one
is used in every term. We fix the convention now, because the paper uses two.
The measurements of Section~\ref{sec:measure} take $J$, the CVaR objective,
throughout: $H$, $\hat S_\pi$ and $\hat S_\theta$ there are CVaR quantities,
and the $\evpi$ they relate to by \eqref{eq:Hrelation} is the CVaR value of
perfect information, not \eqref{eq:evpi}. The enumeration of
Section~\ref{sec:gaps} takes $j$, expected loss, so that its $\evpi$ is
\eqref{eq:evpi} exactly. The premium bound $\ubound$ is stated for expected loss
only and is never applied to a CVaR measurement anywhere in this paper.
Given that convention, $H$ is a planner-specific measurement and is reported
as such throughout.
Section~\ref{sec:gaps} measures both gaps directly on instances where exact
optimisation is possible, and reports how large the discrepancy actually is
and how its sign behaves.
Table~\ref{tab:quantities} fixes the vocabulary.

