\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:applic} collects the assumptions each result needs and where
it applies, since the hypotheses differ from result to result and the
distinctions matter for what may be claimed of the measurements.

\section{Computational verification}\label{sec:verify}

The experiments in this section are not offered as proof of the analytical
results. They serve two purposes: verifying that the implementation matches
the definitions, and searching finite instances for violations that would
reveal errors in the stated results. Every instance is enumerated exactly, over all plans and all $\theta$, with no
sampling inside an instance; seeds are used only to draw the instances
themselves. The pipeline is
deterministic and reproduced identical reported outputs on an independent
machine.

\begin{table}[ht]
\caption{Enumeration checks. A single violation would falsify the
corresponding statement.}\label{tab:verify}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrlr@{}}
\toprule
batch & inst. & viol. & auxiliary check & tightness \\
\midrule
generic random & 6000 & 0 & --- & mean 0.143, max 0.985 \\
Thm.~\ref{thm:zero} regime & 2000 & 0 & nonzero-EVPI cases: 0 & --- \\
Cor.~\ref{cor:dilution} regime & 2000 & 0 & formula mismatches: 0 & mean 0.221, max 1.000 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:verify} reports the three batches, and Figure~\ref{fig:tight}
shows the distribution of the ratio of exact EVPI to the bound. Most instances lie near zero, so the bound is loose in general.
The dilution batch is loose too, with mean ratio $0.221$: the premium is
exact there, but the bound is not. Equality is reached only on some of its
instances, and it holds exactly in the sub-case identified after
Corollary~\ref{cor:dilution}, so the bound cannot be multiplied by any
constant smaller than one without further assumptions.

\begin{figure}[ht]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=0.78\textwidth]{fig4_tightness.png}
\caption{Ratio of exact EVPI to the bound of Theorem~\ref{thm:premium} over
6000 enumerated instances. No value exceeds one.}
\label{fig:tight}
\end{figure}

\paragraph{The premium against exact EVPPI.} On miniature disjoint instances
we computed the exact per-channel $\evppi_k$ by enumeration and compared it
with the quantity Corollary~\ref{cor:evppi} says bounds it, namely
$I_k/K_0$. Both sides carry the $1/K_0$ factor, so the slack reported here is
slack in the bound and not the divisor. Over five independent seeds and
$24{,}981$ channels, $I_k/K_0$ never fell below the enumerated $\evppi_k$,
and the whole-instance bound was never violated. Rank agreement is moderate
and stable across seeds (Spearman $0.505$ to $0.516$; Pearson $0.597$ to
$0.615$), with the bound typically about $2.7$ times the quantity it
bounds.

\paragraph{Search for a counterexample to Conjecture~\ref{conj:shared}.}
Over five seeds and $100{,}000$ instances in which channels feed several
focal categories, the conjectured bound was never violated and the minimum
slack was zero, so it is attained with equality on some instances. The
search covers instances with at most three channels and three focal
categories; a counterexample may exist outside that range.

\section{Empirical study}\label{sec:measure}

\subsection{What is real and what is modelled}\label{sec:whatisreal}

The topologies are real. The Willems dataset~\cite{willems2008} is a public
INFORMS/MSOM supplement of multi-echelon supply chain structures. Everything
else is modelled: which candidate is the true supplier, the risk attributes
candidates carry, the probe channel, the disruption correlation structure
and the failure probabilities are all generated by the model of
Section~\ref{sec:model}. No public dataset records observed sub-tier
supplier identities, attributes or probe outcomes. We therefore refer
throughout to benchmark network topologies with model-generated latent
overlays, and no conclusion rests on observed disruption outcomes.

\paragraph{Focal categories.} How $K_0$ is determined depends on the chain.
Where a chain has several sinks, the virtual market focal consumes one
category per end product, so $K_0$ is the number of sinks; where a chain has a
single sink, that sink is itself the focal node and $K_0$ is the number of
input categories it consumes. Across the 32 retained chains, every one of which the loader reports as
multi-sink, $K_0$ ranges from $2$ to $672$ with a median of $52$. The
comparison with the values $K\in\{2,3,4\}$ at which exact enumeration is
possible in Section~\ref{sec:gaps} is therefore not uniform: most chains lie
far above that range, but three of the 32 have $K_0=2$, inside it. At $K=4$,
the largest enumerable value, $H$ already understates $\evpi$ on $63\%$ of
instances, and how the discrepancy behaves at $52$ or $672$ is not known.
That is why the trend of Section~\ref{sec:gaps} is not extrapolated to the
benchmark measurements, in either direction.

One consequence of the construction should be stated here rather than left to
be inferred. The virtual market focal consumes one category per end product
and each end product leaves the chain through exactly one sink, so every focal
category is served by a single candidate by construction, as the census of
Section~\ref{sec:lam0} confirms. The tier-one layer, the one nearest the loss,
is therefore fully known in every instance, and structural uncertainty can
reach the loss only indirectly through a cascade from tier two or deeper. A
design in which the nearest layer is transparent should be expected to measure
a small advantage; that is a property of the construction and not a finding
about supply networks.

\paragraph{The two regimes.} The \emph{public-attribute} regime is
$\lambda=0$: every candidate's risk attribute is common knowledge, and only
the supplier identities are latent. The \emph{latent-attribute} regime is
$\lambda=0.8$: nodes at tier two or deeper additionally carry a
mean-preserving two-point risk attribute, drawn with dispersion $\lambda$
about the node's nominal rate, whose assignment to candidates is hidden until
probed. This overlay is the network analogue of the family
$\mathcal D(\lambda)$ of Definition~\ref{def:disp}, not an instance of it: it
disperses at node level across a cascade rather than within one isolated
category with the three actions of Definition~\ref{def:disp}. Comparisons
between Proposition~\ref{prop:disp} and the measurements are therefore
qualitative, and we treat them as such in Section~\ref{sec:sweep}.

\paragraph{The synthetic family.} Twelve networks drawn from the scale-free
family of the pipeline's generator, at a target size of $1000$ nodes. The
generator allocates that total across tiers and rounds each tier to an
integer, which yields realised sizes of about $977$; correlation regions are
assigned by inheritance from a node's consumer, so a supplier usually shares
its consumer's region. Unlike the benchmark topologies, which are real and carry
a modelled overlay, these networks are generated in full. The generator and
its parameters accompany the code. Twelve is the number of evaluation
networks the pipeline draws per family; it is too few for population claims,
which is why we treat this family as a set of structural stress tests
throughout.

\subsection{Design and preprocessing}\label{sec:design}

\begin{table}[ht]
\caption{Principal parameters of the measured problem. The node cost model,
the nominal failure rates, the candidate pool sizes and the copula
correlation are set in the configuration accompanying the code. The tail
level $\alpha$, the scenario
counts and the mitigation budget $B$ all govern the numbers in
Sections~\ref{sec:lam0}--\ref{sec:money}; $B$ in particular fixes which
actions are affordable and so which breakpoints exist in
\eqref{eq:premium}.}\label{tab:params}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lll@{}}
\toprule
parameter & value & source \\
\midrule
tail level $\alpha$ & $0.9$ & configuration \\
mitigation budget $B$ & $9.0$ & run report, all reported runs \\
scenario draws per evaluation & $160$ & configuration \\
scenario batch used in planning & $200$ & configuration \\
correlation clusters & 6 regions & configuration default \\
seeded evaluations per instance & 10 / 16 / 8 & Tables~\ref{tab:lam0},
\ref{tab:pilot}, \ref{tab:sweep} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Table~\ref{tab:params} lists the parameters of the measured problem. A
cost-budgeted greedy backup-qualification planner is held identical across
all conditions; candidate pools are built only from prior and public
information; the prior over latent structure is sampled and calibrated to the
public sourcing multiplicity marginal, nothing being conditioned on any
observation; evaluation is paired by seed. Planning and evaluation use
disjoint scenario batches (Table~\ref{tab:params}), so every advantage
reported below is measured out of sample with respect to the scenarios the
planner optimised against; a near-zero advantage therefore cannot be
explained by the planner having overfitted its own batch. A leakage audit confirms
that the uninformed planner's output does not change when the hidden ground
truth is permuted. The wider pipeline also implements a probe channel, but
neither $\evpi$ nor $H$ involves probing: the uninformed planner sees only the
prior and the informed planner sees $\theta$ outright, so no probe parameter
enters any number reported below.

\begin{table}[ht]
\caption{Preprocessing of the benchmark collection.}\label{tab:preproc}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrp{6.4cm}@{}}
\toprule
step & networks & criterion \\
\midrule
released collection & 38 & Willems~\cite{willems2008} \\
minimum size filter & $-6$ & fewer than 30 nodes in the focal cone. The six
excluded chains are 01--06, with cones of 9, 14, 18, 23, 28 and 29 nodes;
such chains have too few latent tiers for discovery to have anything to
find \\
retained & 32 & each given a virtual market focal consuming every end
product as its own category when the chain has several sinks \\
evaluated instances & 96 & three seeded overlays per retained network \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

The minimum-size threshold in Table~\ref{tab:preproc} is 30 nodes in the
focal cone. It is the pipeline default and was applied before any outcome was
observed; the retained set is chains 07--38, which is what the
archived result files contain. No chain lies near the threshold: the largest
excluded cone has 29 nodes and the smallest retained one has 39, so the
partition does not depend on the exact value chosen or on whether the virtual
market focal is counted. The rule is not neutral, however: it removes the smallest chains and biases the
retained set towards deeper, larger topologies. Since
Section~\ref{sec:open} discusses which networks show positive value, that
bias should be kept in mind; the excluded chains would, if anything, be
expected to show less value rather than more.

The 96 instances comprise three overlays per underlying network and are
therefore not independent network replicates. Summary statistics are
computed at instance level; reliability is assessed at network level; and
proportions over instances should be read as clustered within 32 networks.

\subsection{Public-attribute regime}\label{sec:lam0}

\begin{table}[ht]
\caption{Heuristic oracle advantage $H$ in the public-attribute regime,
$\lambda=0$.}\label{tab:lam0}
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrrrrr@{}}
\toprule
dataset & inst. & mean & median & exact zeros & $>0.01$ & frac $>0.02$ \\
\midrule
Benchmark topologies & 96 & $+0.0011$ & 0.0000 & 24 & 3 & 0.010 \\
Synthetic $n\!\approx\!977$ & 12 & $+0.0028$ & 0.0000 & 5 & 3 & 0.167 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\end{table}

Six of the 96 benchmark instances have an advantage exceeding twice its own
standard error, computed across the seeded evaluations of that instance, and
the largest single instance reaches $0.0212$. That count is a resolution
statement and should be read as one: with 160 evaluation scenarios at
$\alpha=0.9$ each CVaR estimate averages the worst 16 draws, so 90 of the 96
instances are not separable from zero at that criterion. The count of six is
not easy to read either way: a
one-sided two-standard-error rule applied to 96 draws from a true null flags
about $2.2$ on average, and a Poisson approximation puts $P(X\ge6)$ at about
$0.025$, so six is mildly inconsistent with a global null while remaining
consistent with the advantage being resolvable on only a handful of instances.
The mean is better resolved than any single instance, but not by as much as an
unclustered calculation suggests, and not enough to call it separated from
zero. Taking the 96 instances as independent gives a standard error of
$0.00038$ against a mean of $0.00106$, a ratio of $2.8$, and $2.2$ at
$\lambda=0.8$. Overlays are clustered within chains
(Section~\ref{sec:design}), however, and computed over the 32 chain means the
ratio falls to $1.92$ at $\lambda=0$ and, over the 14 chains, to $1.96$ at
$\lambda=0.8$, two-sided $p=0.064$ and $p=0.072$. The clustered figures are
the ones to read, and on them neither mean is significantly different from
zero at conventional levels. We therefore report means and medians as
descriptions of the distribution rather than as measurements separated from
zero, and the comparisons of Section~\ref{sec:sweep} inherit that
limitation. A quarter
of the instances give exactly zero. Figure~\ref{fig:lam0} shows the
distributions. We offer no explanation of the mass at zero from
Theorem~\ref{thm:zero}. At $\lambda=0$ the construction makes candidates
within a category approximately exchangeable in marginal law, which is not
sufficient for (H1) (Section~\ref{sec:zero}); and even (H1) alone
permits positive EVPI, since the $\theta$-optimal plan may be
identification-dependent and strictly better than every identification-free
plan, so consistency with (H1) predicts nothing about the mass at zero. Nor
is (H2) enforced by construction: the pipeline prices every securing action
by a single cost attached to the node secured, so whether an
identification-free replacement is no dearer than the
identification-dependent action it replaces is decided by the two node costs
and holds for some categories and not others. We can, however, say how far the checkable
route of Proposition~\ref{prop:witness} goes on these topologies, since both
of its conditions are read off the public graph and cost vector. Across the
32 retained chains there are $14{,}977$ categories, of which $13{,}313$
($88.9\%$) have a candidate pool of size one and so carry nothing to
identify: their
contribution to the value of structural information is zero by construction,
not by any theorem. Of the $1{,}664$ genuinely latent categories, $106$
($6.4\%$) satisfy the sole-consumer condition, $210$ ($12.6\%$) satisfy the
cost condition, and $47$ ($2.8\%$) satisfy both, so the witness holds on
$2.8\%$ of them. The first group provably contributes nothing: with one candidate there is no
$\theta$ to learn. The second does not. Proposition~\ref{prop:witness}
establishes (H2) for a category, while Theorem~\ref{thm:zero} is a global
statement that also requires (H1), and no per-category zero-value result
appears anywhere in this paper. The $47$ are categories in which one of the
two hypotheses is verifiably met, and nothing more. None of them sits at the
focal node, as the hypothesis $v\ne0$ of Proposition~\ref{prop:witness}
requires: every focal category in these chains has a candidate pool of size
one. That is forced by the virtual market construction rather than observed
(Section~\ref{sec:whatisreal}), which also makes the hypothesis $v\ne0$ close
to a formality here: the categories the witness cannot reach are exactly the
categories with nothing to identify.

Two things follow, and the second is unwelcome.
Proposition~\ref{prop:witness} does not explain the mass at zero: at $2.8\%$
of latent categories it is too rare to be the mechanism, and the sole-consumer
condition is what binds, as one would expect on chains with shared suppliers.
What does explain a large part of the observed concentration is candidate
multiplicity, or its absence: nearly nine categories in ten in this
collection have one possible supplier. That is a property of the benchmark
collection, not a result about supply networks, and it should temper how far
the measurements of this section are read as evidence about networks in
general. What determines the residual mass at zero among the latent
categories is unidentified, as Section~\ref{sec:limits} concedes, and the
agreement with Theorem~\ref{thm:zero} is not evidence that its conditions
hold.

