\begin{appendices}

\section{The screening score}\label{sec:index}

Theorem~\ref{thm:premium} suggests a quantity a firm could evaluate on its own
network before commissioning discovery work. For each latent channel $(v,k)$
feeding a set $F(v,k)$ of focal categories, let
$w=K_0^{-1}\sum_{f\in F}\min\{1,\bar p_f/(1-\alpha)\}$ with $\bar p_f$ the
prior starvation probability of focal category $f$, the per-category term
supplied by Proposition~\ref{prop:bern}; let $\phi$ be the increase in
$K_0^{-1}\sum_{f\in F}\bar p_f$ when the channel is forced inoperative in the
prior network and everything else is left as it is, one evaluation of the
prior model; and let $I$ be the premium \eqref{eq:premium} on the channel's
finite breakpoint set. The \emph{structural information screening score} is
\begin{equation}
\siss=\sum_{(v,k)} w\cdot\phi\cdot I .
\label{eq:siss}
\end{equation}

The score is placed here rather than in the body because nothing in the paper
depends on it. Each factor $I$ bounds its own channel's partial value by
Corollary~\ref{cor:evppi}, but the aggregate applies the weight and
attribution outside the assumptions of Theorem~\ref{thm:premium}, in
particular to channels feeding several focal categories, and we have no
theorem that the aggregate remains an upper bound there. The scales also
differ: $w$ is a CVaR-scale importance factor while $\phi$ and $I$ are
expected-loss quantities, so the product is on the scale of neither
objective. A pre-specified test of its rank-based predictive validity did not
meet its reliability criterion (Section~\ref{sec:prereg}). We therefore
report it as a ranking device with no demonstrated validity at network level,
and use it nowhere in Sections~\ref{sec:verify}--\ref{sec:money}.

\section{Reproducibility and provenance}\label{app:prov}

\paragraph{Data.} Willems~\cite{willems2008}, public INFORMS/MSOM
supplement, DOI 10.1287/msom.1070.0176. Retained networks and the
preprocessing rule are given in Table~\ref{tab:preproc}.

\paragraph{Determinism.} Seeds derive from a stable checksum of the instance
identifier rather than from any hash whose salt varies between processes, so
every reported figure regenerates exactly from a fixed code revision. Across
revisions the aggregate statistics are reproduced but instance-level values
need not be, since a change of revision can shift the pseudorandom stream.
The verification pipeline reproduced identical reported outputs on an
independent machine.

\paragraph{Frozen result files.} All reported measurements derive from
result files archived at submission, with SHA-256 digests

\begin{itemize}
